          "htmlContent": "\n    <!DOCTYPE html>\n    <html>\n    <head>\n      <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n      <style>\n        * { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }\n        body { font-family: 'HandwriteAI Handwriting', cursive; background: white; }\n        .page-container {\n          width: 794px;\n          height: 1123px;\n          position: relative;\n          padding: 80px 60px 80px 60px;\n          background: white;\n        }\n        .paper-ruled { background-color: white; }\n        \n    @font-face {\n      font-family: 'HandwriteAI Handwriting';\n      src: local('Patrick Hand'), local('Comic Neue'), local('Brush Script MT'), local('Segoe Script'), local('Comic Sans MS');\n      font-display: swap;\n    }\n    @font-face {\n      font-family: 'HandwriteAI Question';\n      src: local('Instrument Serif'), local('Georgia'), local('Times New Roman'), local('Serif');\n      font-display: swap;\n    }\n    .handwriting-text {\n      font-family: 'HandwriteAI Handwriting', 'Patrick Hand', 'Comic Neue', 'Brush Script MT', 'Segoe Script', 'Comic Sans MS', cursive;\n      font-size: 7.285714285714285px;\n      line-height: 141.5529411764706px;\n      letter-spacing: 0.4290999999999999px;\n      word-spacing: 22.095238095238095px;\n      font-weight: 500;\n      transform: skewX(-3.1deg);\n      color: #1e293b;\n    }\n    .question-text {\n      font-family: 'HandwriteAI Question', 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;\n      font-size: 8.014285714285714px;\n      line-height: 169.86352941176472px;\n      font-weight: 600;\n      color: #1e3a5f;\n      transform: skewX(-0.9299999999999999deg);\n    }\n    .margin-line {\n      background-image: linear-gradient(to right, rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.3) 1px, transparent 1px);\n      background-position: 60px 0;\n      background-size: 1px 100%;\n      background-repeat: no-repeat;\n    }\n  \n        .assignment-title {\n          font-family: 'HandwriteAI Question', Georgia, serif;\n          font-size: 9.714285714285714px;\n          font-weight: 600;\n          color: #1e3a5f;\n          margin-bottom: 141.5529411764706px;\n          text-align: center;\n          transform: skewX(-0.6200000000000001deg);\n        }\n        .question-block {\n          margin-bottom: 24px;\n          page-break-inside: avoid;\n        }\n        .question-number {\n          font-weight: 600;\n          margin-right: 8px;\n        }\n        .answer-text {\n          margin-left: 2.5rem;\n        }\n        .page-number {\n          position: absolute;\n          bottom: 30px;\n          right: 30px;\n          font-size: 12px;\n          color: #93c5fd;\n        }\n        @media print {\n          .page-container { page-break-after: always; }\n        }\n      </style>\n    </head>\n    <body>\n      <div class=\"page-container paper-ruled margin-line\">\n        <div class=\"question-block\"><p class=\"question-text\"><span class=\"question-number\">Q1:</span> <p>What is quantum mechanics?</p>\n</p><div class=\"answer-text handwriting-text\"><p>Quantum mechanics is the fundamental branch of physics that describes the physical properties of nature at the scale of atoms and subatomic particles. Unlike classical mechanics, which relies on deterministic trajectories governed by Newton's laws, quantum mechanics is inherently probabilistic. It posits that physical systems are described by a mathematical entity called a wavefunction, typically denoted by <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"\\Psi\">\\(\\Psi\\)</span>, which encapsulates the probability amplitudes of all possible states of a system. The evolution of this wavefunction is governed by the Schrödinger equation, a linear partial differential equation that allows for the phenomenon of superposition, where a particle exists in multiple states simultaneously until a measurement causes the wavefunction to \"collapse\" into a single definite eigenstate.</p>\n<p>Beyond the probabilistic nature of measurement, quantum mechanics introduces foundational concepts that defy classical intuition, most notably wave-particle duality, Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle, and quantum entanglement. Wave-particle duality suggests that entities like electrons and photons exhibit both wave-like and particle-like properties depending on the experimental context. The uncertainty principle establishes a fundamental limit to the precision with which certain pairs of physical properties, such as position and momentum, can be known simultaneously. Furthermore, entanglement describes a quantum phenomenon where the states of multiple particles become inextricably linked such that the measurement of one particle instantaneously influences the state of the other, regardless of the distance separating them. These principles collectively form the bedrock of modern physics, underpinning our understanding of chemical bonding, solid-state physics, and the development of quantum information technologies.</p>\n</div></div>\n        <div class=\"page-number\">Page 2 of 4</div>\n      </div>\n    </body>\n    </html>\n  ",
