          "htmlContent": "\n    <!DOCTYPE html>\n    <html>\n    <head>\n      <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n      <style>\n        * { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }\n        body { font-family: 'HandwriteAI Handwriting', cursive; background: white; }\n        .page-container {\n          width: 794px;\n          height: 1123px;\n          position: relative;\n          padding: 80px 60px 80px 60px;\n          background: white;\n        }\n        .paper-ruled { background-color: white; }\n        \n    @font-face {\n      font-family: 'HandwriteAI Handwriting';\n      src: local('Patrick Hand'), local('Comic Neue'), local('Brush Script MT'), local('Segoe Script'), local('Comic Sans MS');\n      font-display: swap;\n    }\n    @font-face {\n      font-family: 'HandwriteAI Question';\n      src: local('Instrument Serif'), local('Georgia'), local('Times New Roman'), local('Serif');\n      font-display: swap;\n    }\n    .handwriting-text {\n      font-family: 'HandwriteAI Handwriting', 'Patrick Hand', 'Comic Neue', 'Brush Script MT', 'Segoe Script', 'Comic Sans MS', cursive;\n      font-size: 7.285714285714285px;\n      line-height: 141.5529411764706px;\n      letter-spacing: 0.4290999999999999px;\n      word-spacing: 22.095238095238095px;\n      font-weight: 500;\n      transform: skewX(-3.1deg);\n      color: #1e293b;\n    }\n    .question-text {\n      font-family: 'HandwriteAI Question', 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;\n      font-size: 8.014285714285714px;\n      line-height: 169.86352941176472px;\n      font-weight: 600;\n      color: #1e3a5f;\n      transform: skewX(-0.9299999999999999deg);\n    }\n    .margin-line {\n      background-image: linear-gradient(to right, rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.3) 1px, transparent 1px);\n      background-position: 60px 0;\n      background-size: 1px 100%;\n      background-repeat: no-repeat;\n    }\n  \n        .assignment-title {\n          font-family: 'HandwriteAI Question', Georgia, serif;\n          font-size: 9.714285714285714px;\n          font-weight: 600;\n          color: #1e3a5f;\n          margin-bottom: 141.5529411764706px;\n          text-align: center;\n          transform: skewX(-0.6200000000000001deg);\n        }\n        .question-block {\n          margin-bottom: 24px;\n          page-break-inside: avoid;\n        }\n        .question-number {\n          font-weight: 600;\n          margin-right: 8px;\n        }\n        .answer-text {\n          margin-left: 2.5rem;\n        }\n        .page-number {\n          position: absolute;\n          bottom: 30px;\n          right: 30px;\n          font-size: 12px;\n          color: #93c5fd;\n        }\n        @media print {\n          .page-container { page-break-after: always; }\n        }\n      </style>\n    </head>\n    <body>\n      <div class=\"page-container paper-ruled margin-line\">\n        <div class=\"question-block\"><p class=\"question-text\"><span class=\"question-number\">Q2:</span> <p>Explain wave-particle duality with equations.</p>\n</p><div class=\"answer-text handwriting-text\"><p>Wave-particle duality posits that all quantum entities exhibit both wave-like and particle-like properties, a principle fundamentally linked by the de Broglie hypothesis. For a particle with momentum <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"p\">\\(p\\)</span>, its wavelength <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"\\lambda\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span> is defined by the relation <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"\\lambda = h/p\">\\(\\lambda = h/p\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"h\">\\(h\\)</span> is Planck’s constant. This equation bridges the gap between the corpuscular nature of matter, characterized by momentum, and wave-like characteristics, characterized by wavelength. Simultaneously, the energy of a quantum is linked to its frequency <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"\\nu\">\\(\\nu\\)</span> through the Planck-Einstein relation, <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"E = h\\nu\">\\(E = h\\nu\\)</span>. Together, these equations imply that the physical behavior of an entity depends on the experimental apparatus used to observe it; for instance, electrons demonstrate particle-like localized impacts in a cloud chamber, yet exhibit interference patterns characteristic of waves when passed through a double-slit, confirming their behavior is governed by a probability wave function, <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"\\Psi\">\\(\\Psi\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>This duality is further formalized by the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle, <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"\\Delta x \\Delta p \\ge \\hbar/2\">\\(\\Delta x \\Delta p \\ge \\hbar/2\\)</span>, which dictates that one cannot simultaneously define a particle's exact position and momentum. As the wave packet becomes more localized (acting more like a particle), the range of momentum components required to construct that packet increases, demonstrating that the \"particle\" manifestation is essentially a superposition of wave modes. In the quantum mechanical framework, the wave function <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"\\Psi(x,t)\">\\(\\Psi(x,t)\\)</span> encapsulates all physical information, and its squared modulus <span class=\"math-inline\" data-latex=\"|\\Psi|^2\">\\(|\\Psi|^2\\)</span> provides the probability density of locating the particle in space. Thus, the transition from wave to particle behavior is not a physical metamorphosis but a consequence of the measurement process, where the wave function collapses upon interaction, reconciling the continuous nature of wave propagation with the discrete nature of quantum detection.</p>\n</div></div>\n        <div class=\"page-number\">Page 3 of 4</div>\n      </div>\n    </body>\n    </html>\n  ",
